[Applause]  welcome to pft ot on yahoo sports peter  king mike florio some of the big issues  in the national football league and  there was a report that came out  yesterday from the athletic vincent  bonsanor who covers the rams for the  athletic reporting that there has been  discussion among league personnel of the  possibility of the  chargers relocating permanently to  london peter look i'm skeptical about  that as a viable possibility  um what's your first reaction when you  think of  the chargers or really any team  relocating on a permanent basis to  london  i think we're at least four years away  from it i think it would be an  inconvenient  uh  to say the least  um thing right now if the chargers were  to actively consider and i have no idea  whether they are  were to actively consider uh moving to  london instead of los angeles and  there's two important factors at play  here  the nfl has its collective bargaining  agreement uh which obviously will expire  in 2021  early in 2021 so they're working right  now to get a new cba done could that  happen a year or two in advance it could  but usually deals like this don't get  done until the last minute and i've  always felt that there's no way a  collective bargaining agreement could  get done or there's no way a team could  move  to  london  until a collective bargaining agreement  got done because there isn't any  provision  to ask  a player or a team of players to play in  a different country on a different  continent  than to play in the united states in  north america  so the nfl pa is going to have something  to say about this number one  and and in order to get the momentum to  have one of these teams move it's a  gigantic process at the absolute  earliest i think it'd be 2022 but  probably 2023 is more realistic so it's  four years away i just i think it is a  little bit abrupt it would surprise me  if this really had legs but we'll see  what happens and the other thing is mike  the reason that i think that this is  really a cloudy situation is that you  keep hearing  uh that the uh the chargers are not uh  you know having a great experience  selling psl's and trying to fill their  their home games at this new stadium in  los angeles so we'll see what happens  yeah and that that is such a key aspect  to this because there's a 20-year lease  for the chargers to play at the stadium  that rams owner stan cronkie is  developing he's not going to let them  out of that lease every once in a while  we're here oh the chargers go to st  louis they go to san diego they go here  no they're not going anywhere they have  a lease that stan kroenke needs them to  honor because he needs the revenue from  those games to help pay for this 5  billion  5 billion dollar complex that he's  building in inglewood so that takes the  charges out of the mix immediately as  far as i'm concerned then there's the  reality and this isn't mentioned in the  story on the athletic.com website the  jaguars have been viewed as the team  that kind of has the unofficial dibs on  london that if they want to go if shot  khan wants to move the team to london  they're going to find a way to make it  work i still think it is a long way from  happening if it ever happens peter the  other cynical view on this getting this  idea out there now and not attaching it  to the jaguars so people in jacksonville  don't freak out attaching it to a team  where it's completely unrealistic and or  the fan base wouldn't care to have their  team attached to it and the chargers fan  base wouldn't care the idea that that  maybe the nfl is trying to use this  possibility as just another way to  squeeze the union to agree to 17 games  because if you do 17 you get 16 neutral  site games you have eight games that get  played in london and nobody has to move  and maybe as they're trying to get the  players to understand how it's going to  be  it's either going to be 17 games or it's  going to be somebody moves and i don't  think any nfl player wants to be on a  team that plays in london  it's so hard to expand from four mike  over in london because the logistics are  so hard look the green bay packers have  never played in london teams fight  playing in london worse than they fight  being on hard knocks so you're right  there's a lot of roadblocks ahead  check out more p-f-t-o-t on yahoo sports  throughout the 2019 nfl season  [Music]  hi i'm mike tarico and thanks for  watching make sure to hit subscribe for  the latest news and highlights from nbc  sports

